# ROOT CAUSE of a family of crashes hit repeatedly while building this app
# on the real machine, each with a different-looking error but the exact
# same origin: the instant PySide6 finishes loading (the line just
# above), it installs a process-wide import hook that, for every module
# ANYONE imports afterward -- not just this app's own code, every single
# import for the rest of the program's life -- calls
# shibokensupport.feature._mod_uses_pyside on it, which runs
# inspect.getsource() on that module purely to detect an opt-in PySide6
# syntax this app never uses (`from __feature__ import snake_case`,
# etc.). That source-inspection is what breaks, and it's broken by
# different, unrelated modules in different ways depending on how each
# one's loader/source looks to a frozen (PyInstaller-built) app:
#   1. matplotlib.category -> dateutil -> "from six.moves import
#      _thread": AttributeError, six's lazy-submodule loader confuses
#      inspect.getsource()'s frozen-compatible fallback.
#   2. resistance_interpretation.py -> scipy.stats ->
#      _distn_infrastructure.py: NameError ("obj" not defined), a
#      dynamically-built scipy class confuses the same source-inspection
#      in a different way.
# Two earlier, narrower attempts at a fix are recorded here for anyone
# reading this later, because both taught something real:
#   - Attempt 1: temporarily restore Python's true builtins.__import__
#     around just the matplotlib import, then reinstate PySide6's
#     wrapper. Worked in every local test, but NOT on the real machine --
#     confirmed by the user's rebuild still crashing at that exact line.
#     Likely because something in the frozen app already imports PySide6
#     before this script's own code starts, so there was no genuine
#     "before" moment left to capture.
#   - Attempt 2: temporarily disable shibokensupport.feature.
#     _mod_uses_pyside itself (confirmed, unlike attempt 1, to reliably
#     take effect regardless of import-order/timing) around just that one
#     matplotlib import, then restore it. This fixed crash #1 above --
#     confirmed on the real machine -- but crash #2 then surfaced on a
#     completely different, unrelated import (scipy.stats, needed by
#     resistance_interpretation.py) once the temporary window had already
#     closed and the real function was back in place. One-off windows
#     don't scale: almost any of this app's other dependencies could
#     trigger the same fragile probe in its own unique way.
#
# The permanent fix: keep the real detection working (qtawesome/qtpy,
# imported later in this file, genuinely depend on it correctly
# detecting THEM), but make it crash-proof for everyone else by wrapping
# it in a plain try/except -- if the probe can't safely inspect a
# module's source, the correct assumption is simply "this module isn't
# using PySide6's opt-in feature syntax" (true for every module in this
# app's entire dependency tree), not "crash the whole program." Installed
# once here, permanently, for the rest of the app's life -- no restore
# needed, since it behaves identically to the original for every module
# that doesn't crash it, and only changes behavior for the ones that
# would otherwise take the whole app down.
try:
    import shibokensupport.feature as _shiboken_feature
    _real_mod_uses_pyside = _shiboken_feature._mod_uses_pyside

    def _safe_mod_uses_pyside(module):
        try:
            return _real_mod_uses_pyside(module)
        except Exception:
            return False

    _shiboken_feature._mod_uses_pyside = _safe_mod_uses_pyside
except Exception:
    pass

