@dataclasses.dataclass
class ShadowThetaProvider:
    """Optional override for _draw_sim_shadow_visualization's three theta-
    resolution points -- lets a caller substitute a DIFFERENT
    theta list than "this feature's own official passes" at each of the
    three places that function normally reads one, without touching any
    of the actual shadow math or drawing downstream of that resolution
    (which is already fully generic w.r.t. where a theta came from).

    Each field is an optional callable; a field left None means "use the
    official thetas unchanged" for that one resolution point specifically
    (so a caller only needs to override the parts it actually cares
    about). `_draw_sim_shadow_visualization(theta_provider=None)` (every
    pre-existing caller) skips this mechanism entirely -- bit-for-bit
    identical to before this was added.

    finger_thetas(finger_index, official_thetas) -> thetas_to_use
    patch_thetas(official_thetas) -> thetas_to_use
    plate_thetas(finger_index, official_thetas) -> thetas_to_use

    Three more overrides exist, for the CROSS-AXIS
    (width-wise) hatch bands specifically, kept deliberately SEPARATE
    from the three above. This addresses a bug in the
    Shadow Sim view -- _cross_axis_boundaries combines every theta fed to
    it via "the enclosing hull of whichever passes individually still
    bridge the linewidth" (see that function's own docstring), which was
    designed to combine a feature's OWN one-or-two official passes. A
    feature's own on-axis pass always has ZERO cross-axis alignment (by
    definition -- deposited straight down its own resist trench, no
    sideways component) and therefore trivially "survives" with its
    boundary completely unchanged; since the hull always takes the
    WIDEST surviving candidate per edge, that zero-shift pass can never
    lose to a genuinely foreign, off-axis pass's shrink -- it always
    "rescues" the feature back to its full nominal width. Feeding the
    SAME combined own+foreign pool used for the axial resolvers above
    into cross-axis therefore made it mathematically impossible for a
    foreign pass to ever visually narrow (let alone fully close) an
    already-introduced feature, no matter how orthogonal that foreign
    pass's angle was -- this produced two visible symptoms: an
    apparent blue-hatched shadow on the long side of a finger being
    deposited, and patches appearing fully red-hatched during their own
    deposition step. Both symptoms trace back to the SAME structural
    bug: a
    feature's own baseline pass gets tangled into the SAME hull test as
    unrelated foreign passes instead of only being visualized on its
    own). These three hooks let a caller supply a SEPARATE, foreign-only
    theta list for cross-axis specifically -- so a feature's own official
    pass(es) always render their already-correct (usually zero-effect)
    baseline via the resolvers above, while cross-axis independently
    shows only what LATER, OTHER-feature passes would or wouldn't
    additionally contribute. Left unset (every pre-existing caller,
    including the axial-only overrides above on their own), each falls
    back to whatever the axial resolver already resolved -- an exact
    no-op, matching this dataclass's existing convention.

    finger_cross_axis_thetas(finger_index, resolved_thetas) -> thetas_to_use
    patch_cross_axis_thetas(resolved_thetas) -> thetas_to_use
    plate_cross_axis_thetas(finger_index, resolved_thetas) -> thetas_to_use

    Superseding the above: the Shadow Sim view
    (_slideshow_draw_shadow_sim_view, this dataclass's only caller) no
    longer pools multiple thetas per feature at all -- it is a per-step
    snapshot, so every hook it supplies returns either an EMPTY list or a
    list of exactly one theta (this step's own current one, if this step
    is even a deposition step). The own/foreign hull-rescue mechanism
    described above can only bite when two or more thetas are combined
    in the same _cross_axis_boundaries call; with at most one theta ever
    in flight, that failure mode is structurally impossible now, not just
    patched around. These six hooks remain fully generic, though -- a
    future caller that genuinely needs a multi-theta pool (and correctly
    keeps a feature's own on-axis passes out of its cross-axis list) can
    still use them exactly as documented above.
    """
    finger_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None
    patch_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None
    plate_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None
    finger_cross_axis_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None
    patch_cross_axis_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None
    plate_cross_axis_thetas_fn: Optional[object] = None

    def finger_thetas(self, finger_index, official):
        return self.finger_thetas_fn(finger_index, official) if self.finger_thetas_fn else official

    def patch_thetas(self, official):
        return self.patch_thetas_fn(official) if self.patch_thetas_fn else official

    def plate_thetas(self, finger_index, official):
        return self.plate_thetas_fn(finger_index, official) if self.plate_thetas_fn else official

    def finger_cross_axis_thetas(self, finger_index, resolved_thetas):
        return (self.finger_cross_axis_thetas_fn(finger_index, resolved_thetas)
                if self.finger_cross_axis_thetas_fn else resolved_thetas)

    def patch_cross_axis_thetas(self, resolved_thetas):
        return (self.patch_cross_axis_thetas_fn(resolved_thetas)
                if self.patch_cross_axis_thetas_fn else resolved_thetas)

    def plate_cross_axis_thetas(self, finger_index, resolved_thetas):
        return (self.plate_cross_axis_thetas_fn(finger_index, resolved_thetas)
                if self.plate_cross_axis_thetas_fn else resolved_thetas)


# --- Light/Dark theme support ---------------------------------------------
# These 8 constants are the app CHROME colors (window background, panel
# background, body/label text, accent highlight, dim/secondary text,
# warning text, borders, hover backgrounds, and outline strokes). They're
# referenced as bare module globals throughout this file's QSS strings,
# matplotlib draw calls, and qtawesome icon colors -- see _apply_theme()
# for how each of those three mechanisms gets refreshed when these values
# change. Semantic/status colors (role colors, warning/risky colors, wafer
# material shading, etc.) are deliberately left theme-invariant elsewhere
# in this file and in chamber_plotting.py/parametric_junction_view.py/imported_junction_view.py
# -- they already read fine against both a light and dark canvas, and the
# user's instruction was "no physics or functional features actually
# break", so those are treated as protected semantics, not cosmetics.
_DARK_VALUES = dict(
    BG_COLOR="#1e1f26", PANEL_COLOR="#14151b", TEXT_COLOR="#e8e8ef",
    ACCENT_COLOR="#7fb8ff", DIM_COLOR="#8a8d9e", WARN_COLOR="#ff8f6b",
    BORDER_COLOR="#33343f", HOVER_COLOR="#2a2c38", OUTLINE_COLOR="#ffffff",
    GRAPH_TEXT_COLOR="#8ecbff", MILL_DEPOSITED_COLOR="#ff5c5c",
    MILL_EDGE_HIGHLIGHT_COLOR="#ffb020", MILL_HISTORY_COLOR="#c792ea",
)
_LIGHT_VALUES = dict(
    BG_COLOR="#f5f6fa", PANEL_COLOR="#e8eaf0", TEXT_COLOR="#1c1d24",
    ACCENT_COLOR="#2f6fd6", DIM_COLOR="#5b5f70", WARN_COLOR="#c8562f",
    BORDER_COLOR="#c7cad4", HOVER_COLOR="#d7dae4", OUTLINE_COLOR="#1c1d24",
    GRAPH_TEXT_COLOR="#12539e", MILL_DEPOSITED_COLOR="#c1272d",
    MILL_EDGE_HIGHLIGHT_COLOR="#c77700", MILL_HISTORY_COLOR="#7c3fa6",
)
# GRAPH_TEXT_COLOR ("quantum blue"): fixes a contrast bug --
# _draw_measurement_overlay's dimension-callout numbers used a hardcoded,
# theme-INVARIANT gray ("#aaacba") that never adapted for light mode --
# measured contrast ratio against the light-mode canvas is 2.09:1 (WCAG
# AA requires >=4.5:1 for normal text), i.e. genuinely near-invisible in
# light mode. Rather than just
# reusing TEXT_COLOR (the Qt CHROME text color -- menus, buttons, panel
# labels -- recoloring that wholesale
# would be a much bigger, unrelated visual change), this is a new,
# separate constant scoped to matplotlib GRAPH typography only: a
# technological/quantum blue that is darker against a light background and
# lighter against a dark one, rather than the same light-blue-on-white in
# both themes.
# Measured contrast: 9.49:1 dark-on-dark-bg, 7.05:1 light-on-light-bg --
# both comfortably clear WCAG AAA (7:1), well above the old gray's
# 2.09:1 failure in light mode.


# --- Front Page brand mark + circuit backdrop -----------------------------
# Both functions read the theme color constants above as plain module
# globals AT CALL TIME (same convention chamber_plotting.py/parametric_junction_view.py use --
# see _apply_theme()'s docstring), so calling either one again after a
# light/dark toggle regenerates it in the new palette. Nothing here is a
# static asset; both are literal SVG built in Python.

def _build_appicon_svg() -> bytes:
    """The comb-cross app icon: one #arm unit (a line, a pad, a 4-tooth
    comb, and a 5th short trailing tooth -- closer to a real fabricated
    device's own uneven comb than a tidy 4) rotated into four arms, a faint
    5-degree-offset "second pass" echo behind the crisp primary mark
    (what a first shadow-evaporation pass would look like once a second
    is deposited over it), inside a chip-package badge with one corner
    bond pad picked out in the accent color as a quiet pin-1 marker."""
    arm = (
        '<line x1="0" y1="0" x2="56" y2="0" stroke-width="3"/>'
        '<rect x="50" y="-5" width="10" height="10" stroke="none"/>'
        '<line x1="63" y1="-9" x2="63" y2="9" stroke-width="3"/>'
        '<line x1="70" y1="-9" x2="70" y2="9" stroke-width="3"/>'
        '<line x1="77" y1="-9" x2="77" y2="9" stroke-width="3"/>'
        '<line x1="84" y1="-9" x2="84" y2="9" stroke-width="3"/>'
        '<line x1="92" y1="-6" x2="92" y2="6" stroke-width="2.5"/>'
    )
    fiducial = (
        '<rect x="-5" y="-5" width="10" height="10" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>'
        '<g stroke-width="1.5">'
        '<line x1="-5" y1="-13" x2="-5" y2="-8"/><line x1="-13" y1="-5" x2="-8" y2="-5"/>'
        '<line x1="5" y1="-13" x2="5" y2="-8"/><line x1="13" y1="-5" x2="8" y2="-5"/>'
        '<line x1="-5" y1="13" x2="-5" y2="8"/><line x1="-13" y1="5" x2="-8" y2="5"/>'
        '<line x1="5" y1="13" x2="5" y2="8"/><line x1="13" y1="5" x2="8" y2="5"/>'
        '</g>'
    )
    echo = "".join(f'<g transform="rotate({a})">{arm}</g>' for a in (50, 140, 230, 320))
    primary = "".join(f'<g transform="rotate({a})">{arm}</g>' for a in (45, 135, 225, 315))
    svg = f'''<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 200 200">
      <rect x="3" y="3" width="194" height="194" rx="34" fill="{PANEL_COLOR}"/>
      <rect x="3" y="3" width="194" height="194" rx="34" fill="none" stroke="{BORDER_COLOR}" stroke-width="2"/>
      <rect x="13" y="13" width="174" height="174" rx="25" fill="none" stroke="{BORDER_COLOR}" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.5"/>
      <rect x="9" y="9" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5" fill="{ACCENT_COLOR}"/>
      <rect x="184" y="9" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5" fill="{BORDER_COLOR}"/>
      <rect x="9" y="184" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5" fill="{BORDER_COLOR}"/>
      <rect x="184" y="184" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5" fill="{BORDER_COLOR}"/>
      <g transform="translate(100,100)" stroke="{DIM_COLOR}" fill="{DIM_COLOR}" opacity="0.55">{echo}</g>
      <g transform="translate(100,100)" stroke="{TEXT_COLOR}" fill="{TEXT_COLOR}">{primary}{fiducial}</g>
    </svg>'''
    return svg.encode("utf-8")


def _build_circuit_backdrop_svg(w: int = 1200, h: int = 480) -> bytes:
    """Full-bleed Manhattan-routed circuit-trace pattern for the Front
    Page hero -- deliberately monotone (BORDER_COLOR at partial opacity,
    on a PANEL_COLOR/BG_COLOR fade) so it reads as texture, not a second
    logo. A radial vignette fades the traces back to solid panel color
    toward the center, where the app icon and name sit on top -- that's
    the "nestle the mark within it" effect, not a separate mask."""
    motifs = []
    cols, rows = 9, 4
    spacing_x, spacing_y = w / cols, h / (rows + 0.5)
    for row in range(rows):
        for col in range(cols):
            cx = spacing_x * (col + 0.5) + (spacing_x * 0.25 if row % 2 else 0.0)
            cy = spacing_y * (row + 0.85)
            rot = 90 * ((row + col) % 4)
            motifs.append(
                f'<g transform="translate({cx:.0f},{cy:.0f}) rotate({rot})">'
                '<line x1="-24" y1="0" x2="6" y2="0"/>'
                '<line x1="6" y1="0" x2="6" y2="14"/>'
                '<line x1="6" y1="14" x2="37" y2="14"/>'
                '<rect x="37" y="11" width="6" height="6" stroke="none"/>'
                '<circle cx="6" cy="14" r="2.2" stroke="none"/>'
                '</g>'
            )
    traces = "".join(motifs)
    svg = f'''<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 {w} {h}" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice">
      <defs>
        <linearGradient id="fpBgFade" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
          <stop offset="0" stop-color="{PANEL_COLOR}"/>
          <stop offset="1" stop-color="{BG_COLOR}"/>
        </linearGradient>
        <radialGradient id="fpVignette" cx="50%" cy="42%" r="60%">
          <stop offset="0" stop-color="{PANEL_COLOR}" stop-opacity="1"/>
          <stop offset="55%" stop-color="{PANEL_COLOR}" stop-opacity="0.82"/>
          <stop offset="100%" stop-color="{PANEL_COLOR}" stop-opacity="0"/>
        </radialGradient>
      </defs>
      <rect width="{w}" height="{h}" fill="url(#fpBgFade)"/>
      <g stroke="{BORDER_COLOR}" fill="{BORDER_COLOR}" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.55">{traces}</g>
      <rect width="{w}" height="{h}" fill="url(#fpVignette)"/>
    </svg>'''
    return svg.encode("utf-8")


